2.2  Cores, threads and sockets

TL;DR
  - A core is a complete processor. A chip (package, or socket) holds several; a
    server board can hold several chips.
  - With SMT (Intel calls it Hyper-Threading), one core runs two hardware
    threads. Each shows up as a CPU, but the two share the core's units: expect
    far less than twice the speed.
  - Logical CPUs = sockets × cores × threads per core. That's what nproc, top
    and the load average count.

      lscpu | grep -E '^(CPU\(s\)|Thread|Core|Socket)'
      nproc                # CPUs this process may use

On your machine
  CPUs      8 logical CPUs on 4 cores, up to 2 threads per core (SMT)

Pitfalls
  - A load average of 8 on "8 CPUs" that are 4 cores with SMT is a busier
    machine than it looks (18.1).
  - In a container limited with --cpus=2, nproc still shows every CPU of the
    host: the limit is a time quota, not fewer CPUs.
  - Recent Intel chips mix performance (P) and efficiency (E) cores, like ARM's
    big.LITTLE: the same task runs at different speeds depending on where the
    scheduler puts it.
  - Benchmarks vary between runs partly because of this: pin them with
    taskset -c 2 ./bench.

Deep dive: 2 questions. Read them with idk 2.2 --why

Related
  Commands  nproc, lscpu
  Paths     /sys/devices/system/cpu/
  Hub       idk cpu CPU
